  <h1>Types of FMLA Violations</h1>
  <p><em>Reviewed by Riley Chen (RC), Employment &amp; Civil Rights Practice Lead. Updated May 2026.</em></p>
  <h2>Interference (29 U.S.C. § 2615(a)(1))</h2><p>The broadest category. Any action that restrains, denies, or interferes with the exercise of FMLA rights. Includes: refusing to approve qualifying leave, discouraging leave-taking, failing to notify employees of FMLA eligibility, counting FMLA absences in attendance systems, and terminating employees immediately upon return from leave.</p>
  <h2>Retaliation (29 U.S.C. § 2615(a)(2))</h2><p>Adverse employment action because an employee exercised FMLA rights: termination, demotion, negative performance evaluation, reduction in pay or hours, exclusion from meetings, reassignment to less desirable duties. Timing is critical &mdash; adverse action within days or weeks of FMLA leave creates a strong inference of retaliation.</p>
  <h2>Notice and designation failures</h2><p>Employers must: notify employees of eligibility within 5 business days of learning of a qualifying reason; provide rights and responsibilities notice; designate qualifying leave as FMLA within 5 business days. Failure to timely designate FMLA can estop the employer from counting the leave against the 12-week entitlement.</p>
  <h2>Intermittent leave abuse allegations</h2><p>Employers sometimes accuse employees of abusing intermittent FMLA by calling out on Mondays/Fridays or before major projects. Even if suspicious, the employer cannot discipline for FMLA-designated absences without medical certification establishing that the pattern is inconsistent with the stated condition. Retaliation for perceived abuse is the most common FMLA dispute.</p>
